**1. Doc comment softened** — `ToolBridge._writeFile` (`lib/src/js/tool_bridge.dart`) no longer claims blanket `Java FileTools.writeFile parity`. It now scopes the claim to the *parent-directory behavior* only:

```dart
/// gh-361: parent-directory behavior of Java `FileTools.writeFile` —
/// creates missing parent directories before writing, ...
///
/// Parity is scoped to that parent creation only: Java `writeFile` also
/// normalizes the path and rejects anything outside the working
/// directory ("Path traversal attempt blocked"), and this bridge — like
/// the whole `file_*` family here — has no such containment check
/// (pre-existing gap, tracked in #365).
```

The matching claims in the test file (group title, inline comment) were scoped the same way, so neither the doc nor the tests can be cited as full method parity later.

**2. Follow-up ticket filed** — [#365 "Sandbox the JS-bridge file_* tool family: reject paths outside the job working directory (Java FileTools traversal-check parity)"](https://github.com/epam/dmtools-dart/issues/365). It covers the whole family (`file_read`/`file_write`/`file_write_lines`/`file_append`/`file_list`/`file_exists`/`file_delete`/`file_mkdir`) on both the bridge and the async `FileToolExecutor` surface, and proposes reusing `validateWithinAllowedBase` / `pathIsWithin` / `gitRepositoryRoot` from `lib/src/integrations/cli/allowed_base.dart` — the exact job-base/git-root/tmpdir semantics `cli_execute_command` already enforces.

